// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["ii-a"] = {"qs": [["Processes related to Depositories", "After confirming a demat request, what does the RTA do with the physical certificates?", ["Returns them to the investor for safe keeping", "Sends them to the stock exchange for storage", "Keeps them active in case the investor later wants to sell in physical form", "Cancels and mutilates them and records the depository as the holder of those shares"], 3, "Once confirmed, the certificates are cancelled and mutilated so they cannot be reused, and the register of members is updated to show the depository as the registered owner."], ["Investor Interface with the R&T Agent", "To issue duplicate securities in place of lost share certificates, an RTA typically requires:", ["Only a phone call from the registered mobile number", "A no-objection letter from the stock exchange", "Form ISR-4 along with an affidavit and an indemnity from the holder", "A letter from any other shareholder of the company"], 2, "Because duplicates replace lost certificates, the holder must protect the company against misuse through an affidavit and indemnity, with other documents depending on value. The RTA then issues a letter of confirmation."], ["Introduction to Securities", "In India, the term 'securities' for the purposes of the securities market is defined in which law?", ["Indian Contract Act, 1872, read with the Companies Act, 2013", "Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956", "Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881", "Sale of Goods Act, 1930"], 1, "Section 2(h) of the Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956 defines securities to include shares, debentures, units of collective schemes, government securities and other instruments. The other laws deal with contracts, cheques and goods, not securities."], ["Processes related to Public Offering of Shares", "Before finalising the basis of allotment, the Registrar to the issue reconciles:", ["The issuer's audited accounts with the lead manager's due diligence report", "Share prices on the grey market with the final issue price", "Bid details from the stock exchanges with fund-blocking data from SCSBs and the sponsor bank", "The list of promoters with the latest shareholding pattern"], 2, "Only bids backed by successfully blocked funds are valid. The registrar matches electronic bid files from the exchanges with blocking confirmations from banks and rejects unmatched or deficient bids."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "A bond that gives the issuer the right to redeem it before maturity at a specified price is a:", ["Puttable bond", "Callable bond", "Convertible bond", "Step-up bond"], 1, "A call option belongs to the issuer, who may redeem early, often when rates fall. A put option belongs to the investor. Convertible bonds convert into shares, and step-up bonds have rising coupons."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "Investing a fixed amount in a scheme every month through an SIP helps the investor mainly by:", ["Guaranteeing a minimum return on the amount invested each month", "Removing all market risk from equity investments", "Buying more units when NAV is low and fewer when it is high", "Avoiding the need to complete KYC requirements"], 2, "This is rupee cost averaging: a fixed amount buys more units at lower NAVs, which can lower the average cost over time. An SIP does not guarantee returns or remove market risk."], ["Modes of Allotment other than Public Offers", "Before making a preferential allotment of equity shares, a listed company must obtain:", ["Only a board resolution", "Prior approval of the Registrar of Companies for the issue price", "Shareholders' approval by a special resolution", "Consent of the depositories"], 2, "A preferential issue needs a special resolution of shareholders under Section 62(1)(c) and the ICDR norms, apart from board approval and in-principle approval from the stock exchanges."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "SEBI's power to issue directions to an intermediary in the interest of investors or the orderly development of the market comes mainly from:", ["Section 12 of the SEBI Act", "Section 15Z of the SEBI Act", "Section 42 of the Companies Act", "Section 11B of the SEBI Act"], 3, "Section 11B empowers SEBI to issue directions to intermediaries and others to protect investors. Section 12 deals with registration of intermediaries, Section 15Z with appeals to the Supreme Court."], ["Client Servicing", "An investor phones the RTA to ask about the record date for an upcoming dividend. How should this be treated?", ["As a complaint, to be reported on SCORES", "As a transmission request needing documents", "As a query, to be answered promptly with accurate information", "As a request to be referred to SEBI"], 2, "A request for information with no allegation of deficiency is a query. Only grievances about service failure, such as non-receipt of dividend, are complaints recorded and redressed as such."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "Units of a Real Estate Investment Trust (REIT) give investors exposure mainly to:", ["Undeveloped agricultural land", "Residential housing loans", "Completed, income-generating commercial real estate", "Shares of listed real estate brokers and housing agents"], 2, "SEBI's REIT rules require most assets to be in completed, rent-generating properties such as office parks, and a large share of distributable cash flow to be paid out to unit holders."], ["Depository Services", "An investor who wants to hold shares in electronic form opens an account with a:", ["Registrar to the issue", "Depository participant", "Credit rating agency", "Clearing corporation"], 1, "Investors cannot open accounts directly with NSDL or CDSL; they open a demat account through a SEBI-registered depository participant such as a bank or broker that is an agent of the depository."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "A bond whose coupon is reset every six months based on a benchmark rate plus a fixed spread is a:", ["Floating rate bond", "Zero-coupon bond", "Deep discount bond", "Fixed rate bond"], 0, "In a floating rate bond, the coupon moves with a reference rate such as a T-bill yield, plus a spread. Zero-coupon and deep discount bonds pay no periodic interest, and fixed rate bonds keep the same coupon."], ["Client Servicing", "SEBI requires RTAs to display an 'investor charter' on their websites. Its main purpose is to:", ["Publish the RTA's annual profit and loss account", "List all shares held by the RTA's employees", "Advertise new IPOs managed by the RTA", "Tell investors about services, timelines, their rights and the grievance process"], 3, "The investor charter sets out services offered, expected timelines, investors' rights and duties, and how to escalate grievances. It aims to make service standards transparent, not to publish financials or adverts."], ["Introduction to Securities", "Which of the following is a money market instrument rather than a capital market instrument?", ["A 10-year government bond", "An equity share of a listed company", "A five-year non-convertible debenture", "A 91-day treasury bill"], 3, "Money market instruments have an original maturity of up to one year and are used for short-term funding. A 91-day T-bill qualifies; long-dated bonds, debentures and equity shares are capital market instruments."], ["Processes related to Depositories", "Anil wants to gift 50 shares from his demat account to his sister's demat account. He instructs his DP using:", ["A demat request form", "A remat request form", "A transmission request form signed by both of them", "An off-market delivery instruction"], 3, "Gifts and other non-exchange transfers are made as off-market transfers using a delivery instruction slip or its online equivalent, giving the reason for transfer. Demat, remat and transmission forms have other uses."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "Participating preference shares give the holder, in addition to the fixed dividend:", ["A right to share in surplus profits and, if so provided, in surplus assets on winding up", "The right to convert into debentures at any time", "Voting rights equal to equity holders in all matters at all times, including routine resolutions", "An exemption from tax on the dividend received each year"], 0, "Participating preference shares share in surplus profits after equity holders get a specified return, and may share surplus assets on winding up. Voting rights for preference holders arise only in limited cases."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "Within an RTA, monitoring compliance with SEBI rules and the redressal of investor grievances is the responsibility of the:", ["Statutory auditor appointed by the members", "Compliance officer", "Debenture trustee", "Lead manager"], 1, "SEBI requires every RTA to appoint a compliance officer to monitor compliance with laws and regulations and to oversee investor grievance redressal. Auditors, trustees and lead managers have other roles."], ["Secondary Market Transactions", "A selling broker fails to deliver shares on the settlement day. To meet the buyer's entitlement, the clearing corporation normally:", ["Cancels the buyer's trade and refunds the brokerage only", "Conducts an auction to buy the shares, or closes out if the auction fails", "Asks the issuer to print new shares for the buyer", "Waits until the seller delivers, however long it takes"], 1, "Short deliveries are handled through an auction in which shares are bought for the buyer, with the cost recovered from the defaulting seller. If the auction fails, the trade is closed out at a prescribed price."], ["Depository Services", "What is the maximum number of holders allowed in a single demat account?", ["Three", "Two", "Four, if all are resident individuals", "Five"], 0, "A demat account may be held by one person singly or by up to three joint holders. The order of names in the account must match the order on securities for demat requests to be accepted."], ["Roles and Responsibilities in a Public Issue", "Once allotment is finalised, the blocked amounts of successful ASBA bidders are transferred to the:", ["Investor Education and Protection Fund", "Depository's settlement account", "Public issue account", "Lead manager's current account"], 2, "On instructions from the registrar, SCSBs and the sponsor bank debit the blocked funds for successful bids and transfer them to the public issue account opened by the issuer with the banker to the issue."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "Closed-ended mutual fund schemes are required to list their units on a stock exchange mainly because:", ["Listing allows the scheme to raise fresh money from the public on every trading day until maturity", "Exchange trading removes the need to calculate the NAV of the scheme", "Only listed schemes are allowed to invest in equity shares", "Investors cannot redeem units with the fund before maturity, so listing provides an exit"], 3, "A closed-ended scheme has a fixed maturity and does not offer continuous redemption. Listing on an exchange gives investors liquidity before maturity. NAV must still be computed and disclosed."], ["Public Offer of Securities", "A red herring prospectus differs from the final prospectus mainly because it does not contain:", ["The objects of the issue and the planned deployment of funds", "The risk factors relating to the issuer", "Complete details of the final issue price and the number of shares offered", "The names of the lead managers"], 2, "The RHP is used during book building, before the price is discovered, so the final price and quantity are left out. Objects, risk factors and intermediaries are disclosed in the RHP."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "A debenture backed by a charge on specific assets of the issuer is called a:", ["Naked debenture", "Secured debenture", "Perpetual debenture", "Zero-coupon debenture"], 1, "When a charge is created on the issuer's assets in favour of debenture holders, the debenture is secured. A naked debenture is unsecured, while perpetual and zero-coupon describe maturity and interest features."], ["Processes related to Public Offering of Shares", "Which of the following is a ground for technical rejection of an IPO application?", ["Bidding at the cut-off price as a retail individual investor in the issue", "Bidding for the minimum lot size", "Applying through a registered broker instead of a bank", "Multiple bids made under the same PAN in the retail category"], 3, "Multiple applications under one PAN in the same category are treated as multiple bids and liable to rejection. Bidding at cut-off, bidding for the minimum lot and using a broker are all permitted."], ["Secondary Market Transactions", "An investor buys shares on the exchange and wants to check that delivery has been completed. Which record should she verify?", ["Her demat account statement or holding balance with the DP", "The company's register of members for physical holders", "The lead manager's allotment list", "The RTA's list of unclaimed dividends"], 0, "Exchange-traded purchases are delivered by credit to the buyer's demat account, so the DP holding statement confirms receipt. The company's physical register and IPO allotment lists are not relevant here."], ["Public Offer of Securities", "A fresh-issue IPO closes with valid bids for only 82% of the issue size. What follows?", ["The issue fails for not meeting minimum subscription, and blocked amounts are released", "Shares are allotted to the 82% and the remaining shares lapse permanently", "The lead manager must extend the bid period until full 100% subscription is reached", "The issuer may proceed if the promoters bring in the shortfall later"], 0, "ICDR requires minimum subscription of 90% of the fresh issue. If it is not received, no allotment is made and all application money must be unblocked or refunded within the prescribed time."], ["Secondary Market Transactions", "Under the T+1 rolling settlement cycle, the ex-date for a corporate action such as a dividend is:", ["One working day before the record date", "The same day as the record date", "Two working days after the record date", "The date of the board meeting"], 1, "Since a buyer on T receives shares on T+1, a buyer on the record date would not be on the record. So with T+1, the ex-date and record date fall on the same day."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "A company issues equity shares of face value ₹10 at ₹150 per share. How much per share is credited to the securities premium account?", ["₹150", "₹140", "₹10", "₹160"], 1, "Only the face value of ₹10 goes to share capital. The excess of the issue price over face value, ₹150 − ₹10 = ₹140, is credited to the securities premium account."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "A company consolidates its shares of face value ₹2 each into shares of face value ₹10 each. An investor holding 1,250 shares will now hold:", ["6,250 shares", "125 shares", "2,500 shares", "250 shares"], 3, "Consolidation is the reverse of a split. Five shares of ₹2 become one share of ₹10, so 1,250 ÷ 5 = 250 shares. The total face value of ₹2,500 remains unchanged."], ["Processes related to Public Offering of Shares", "A retail category in an IPO is heavily oversubscribed, so not every applicant can receive the minimum bid lot. How is allotment made?", ["Every applicant gets a proportionate fraction of a lot rounded down", "The minimum bid lot is allotted to as many applicants as possible, chosen by draw of lots", "Applicants who bid the largest amounts are allotted shares first, in descending order of amount", "Applications received earliest in the bid period are preferred"], 1, "ICDR aims to give the minimum bid lot to the maximum number of retail individual investors. Where that is not possible for all, successful applicants are selected by lottery in consultation with the designated exchange."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "An investor redeems 500 units of a scheme when the NAV is ₹40. The exit load is 1%. What amount will he receive (ignoring tax)?", ["₹20,000", "₹19,800", "₹20,200", "₹19,600"], 1, "Gross value is 500 × ₹40 = ₹20,000. An exit load of 1% reduces the redemption price to ₹39.60, so the investor receives 500 × ₹39.60 = ₹19,800."], ["Processes related to Depositories", "After an investor submits a demat request, the shares are credited to her demat account only when:", ["The DP receives the original certificates by post from the investor", "The issuer's RTA confirms the request electronically to the depository", "The stock exchange approves the request", "The investor sells the shares in the market"], 1, "The DP forwards the request electronically and sends certificates to the RTA. Only after the RTA verifies and confirms does the depository credit the beneficial owner's account."], ["Investor Interface with the R&T Agent", "Shares on which dividend has been unclaimed for seven consecutive years were transferred to the IEPF. How can the shareholder claim them back?", ["By filing Form IEPF-5 online with the IEPF Authority", "By submitting Form ISR-1 to the company's RTA", "By asking the DP to reverse the transfer", "The shares cannot be claimed once transferred"], 0, "Shares and unclaimed amounts transferred to the IEPF can be claimed by filing Form IEPF-5, with documents verified by the company. Neither the RTA nor the DP can reverse the transfer directly."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "Which intermediary is appointed to protect the interests of holders of listed debentures and to monitor the security created for them?", ["Registrar to the issue", "Credit rating agency", "Banker to the issue", "Debenture trustee"], 3, "A SEBI-registered debenture trustee acts for debenture holders, monitors asset cover and can enforce security on default. The registrar maintains records, the rating agency rates the issue and the banker handles funds."], ["Public Offer of Securities", "In a book-built IPO, the floor price is ₹300. What is the highest cap price permitted under SEBI's ICDR rules?", ["₹330", "₹315", "₹390", "₹360"], 3, "The cap price cannot exceed 120% of the floor price, so the maximum is ₹300 × 1.2 = ₹360. ₹315 is the minimum cap, because the cap must be at least 105% of the floor."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "In mutual fund records, a 'folio' is:", ["An account number identifying an investor's holdings with a fund house", "The list of securities held by a mutual fund scheme on a given date", "The daily NAV report sent by the AMC to SEBI", "The certificate issued to a distributor by AMFI"], 0, "A folio number identifies an investor's account with an AMC; several schemes can be held under one folio. The list of securities held is the scheme's portfolio."], ["Client Servicing", "A company's dividend warrant remains unpaid or unclaimed 30 days after declaration. The unpaid amount must be transferred to:", ["The company's general reserve", "The Investor Education and Protection Fund immediately", "The RTA's own current account", "A special unpaid dividend account of the company with a scheduled bank"], 3, "Section 124 of the Companies Act requires unpaid dividends to be moved to an Unpaid Dividend Account within seven days after the 30-day period. Only amounts unclaimed for seven years go to the IEPF."], ["Modes of Allotment other than Public Offers", "Under the Companies Act, 2013, a further issue of shares offered to existing equity shareholders in proportion to their holding is a:", ["Preferential allotment", "Rights issue", "Qualified institutions placement", "Offer for sale"], 1, "Section 62(1)(a) provides for offering new shares to existing shareholders in proportion to their paid-up holding, which is a rights issue. Preferential allotments and QIPs go to selected investors."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "An investor holds some mutual fund units in a demat account and some in non-demat folios. Who sends him the single Consolidated Account Statement?", ["Each AMC separately for its own schemes only, through its own RTA", "The stock exchange where he trades", "The depository with which his demat account is held", "SEBI through the SCORES portal"], 2, "Under SEBI norms, investors with a demat account receive a single CAS from the depository covering both demat holdings and MF folios. Investors without demat accounts receive CAS from the MF RTAs."], ["Depository Services", "Which demat account holder cannot make a nomination for the account?", ["A body corporate", "A sole individual holder", "Individual joint holders", "A resident individual holding shares singly"], 0, "Nomination is available to individuals holding singly or jointly. Non-individuals such as companies, trusts and HUFs cannot nominate, since succession rules on death do not apply to them."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "Equity shares that carry voting rights different from those of ordinary equity shares are known as:", ["Shares with differential voting rights", "Sweat equity shares", "Bonus shares", "Partly paid-up shares with unpaid calls outstanding"], 0, "DVR shares give more or fewer votes per share than ordinary shares. Sweat equity is issued for know-how or services, bonus shares are issued free from reserves, and partly paid shares relate to unpaid calls."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "SEBI may levy monetary penalties on an intermediary for failures such as not redressing investor grievances. These penalties are imposed by:", ["An adjudicating officer appointed under the SEBI Act", "The stock exchange's disciplinary committee", "The Registrar of Companies", "The Securities Appellate Tribunal acting on its own motion"], 0, "Chapter VIA of the SEBI Act provides for penalties imposed after an adjudication by an officer appointed by SEBI. SAT hears appeals against such orders rather than imposing penalties itself."], ["Processes related to Depositories", "A sole demat account holder dies, leaving a registered nominee. How are the shares transmitted?", ["The RTA issues physical certificates in the nominee's name", "The nominee must buy the shares on the exchange", "The shares are cancelled and the company pays their face value", "The DP transfers them to the nominee's demat account on receiving the required documents"], 3, "The nominee submits a transmission request form and death certificate to the deceased's DP. After verification, the securities are transmitted to the nominee's demat account; the nominee needs a demat account."], ["Client Servicing", "Which of the following is an example of a complaint rather than a query?", ["An investor asks for the RTA's office address", "An investor reports that a declared dividend was not credited to his bank account", "An investor asks when the AGM will be held", "An investor asks for a copy of the nomination form"], 1, "A complaint alleges a service failure needing redressal, such as non-receipt of a dividend. Requests for addresses, dates or forms are queries and can be answered directly."], ["Roles and Responsibilities in a Public Issue", "Apart from the branches of SCSBs, an investor can submit a physical ASBA bid form at:", ["Designated locations of syndicate members, registered brokers, RTAs and CDPs", "Any office of the Registrar of Companies in the issuer's state", "The issuer's registered office only", "Any branch of SEBI"], 0, "SEBI widened bid collection to syndicate members, registered stock brokers, registrars and collecting DPs at designated centres. They upload bids to the exchange platform and forward forms for blocking of funds."], ["Introduction to Securities", "Meena buys 100 shares of a listed company on the stock exchange from another investor. Which statement about this trade is correct?", ["The company receives no money; the price is paid to the selling investor", "The company receives the price as fresh capital for its business plans", "The RTA collects the price and credits it to the company's bank account", "The exchange keeps the price until the company's next annual general meeting"], 0, "A secondary market trade is between investors. The selling investor receives the price; the issuer raises no new money. Companies receive money only when they issue fresh securities in the primary market."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "Depository receipts issued by an Indian company and listed on a stock exchange in the United States are called:", ["Indian Depository Receipts", "Foreign Currency Convertible Bonds", "American Depository Receipts", "Masala bonds"], 2, "ADRs represent underlying shares of a non-US company and trade in the US. IDRs are issued by foreign companies in India, FCCBs are convertible bonds, and masala bonds are rupee-denominated bonds issued abroad."], ["Processes related to Public Offering of Shares", "Anchor investors in a book-built IPO submit their bids:", ["On the last day of the bid period", "One working day before the issue opens to the public", "On the day of listing", "After the basis of allotment is finalised by the registrar"], 1, "The anchor investor bid/issue period is one working day before the issue opening date. Allocation to anchors is disclosed before the public issue opens, which signals institutional interest."], ["Secondary Market Transactions", "A deal in a single scrip in which the total quantity bought or sold by a client exceeds 0.5% of the company's listed equity shares is called a:", ["Block deal", "Spot deal", "Bulk deal", "Odd-lot deal"], 2, "Trades by a client in a day totalling more than 0.5% of listed equity shares are bulk deals and must be disclosed by the exchanges. Block deals are large trades executed in a separate window."], ["Secondary Market Transactions", "A stock broker must issue a contract note to a client for trades executed on an exchange within:", ["Seven days of the trade", "One month of the trade", "The next quarterly statement", "24 hours of the trade"], 3, "SEBI requires brokers to issue contract notes, physically or electronically, within 24 hours of execution. The note records price, quantity, brokerage and charges, and is evidence of the trade."], ["Introduction to Securities", "The legal framework that allows securities to be held and transferred in electronic book-entry form in India is provided mainly by:", ["The Information Technology Act, 2000", "The Banking Regulation Act, 1949", "The Payment and Settlement Systems Act, 2007", "The Depositories Act, 1996"], 3, "The Depositories Act, 1996 enabled depositories to hold securities in dematerialised form and made the depository the registered owner, with investors as beneficial owners. The other Acts cover electronic records, banks and payment systems."], ["Public Offer of Securities", "In a fixed price issue, unlike a book-built issue, the investor:", ["Bids within a band and the price is found later", "Knows the exact issue price before applying", "Can bid at the cut-off price only", "Pays only after the shares are listed"], 1, "In a fixed price issue the price is stated in the offer document, so investors apply at that price. In book building investors bid within a band and the final price is discovered from demand."], ["Client Servicing", "Under SEBI's SCORES system, an entity receiving an investor complaint must resolve it and file an action taken report within:", ["7 calendar days of receipt", "21 calendar days of receipt", "45 calendar days of receipt", "90 calendar days of receipt"], 1, "SEBI's revised SCORES framework requires the entity to redress the complaint and upload an action taken report within 21 calendar days. Delays are escalated and monitored by the designated body and SEBI."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "A company has 8% cumulative preference shares. It paid no preference dividend for two years and now has profits to distribute. Which is correct?", ["Arrears lapse and only the current year's preference dividend is payable", "Equity holders are paid first and preference holders receive the balance", "Preference holders must convert their shares into equity to claim arrears", "Arrears of preference dividend must be paid before any equity dividend"], 3, "With cumulative preference shares, unpaid dividends accumulate as arrears. Before any dividend is paid to equity holders, the arrears and the current preference dividend must be cleared. Arrears lapse only for non-cumulative shares."], ["Investor Interface with the R&T Agent", "A holder of physical shares wants to register his PAN, mobile number and email address with the RTA. Which form should he submit?", ["Form ISR-4", "Form SH-13", "Form ISR-1", "Form ISR-5"], 2, "SEBI's common forms use ISR-1 for registering or updating PAN, KYC and contact details. ISR-4 is for service requests like duplicate securities, SH-13 for nomination and ISR-5 for transmission."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "A bond is trading in the market below its face value. Its yield to maturity will be:", ["Higher than its coupon rate", "Lower than its coupon rate", "Exactly equal to its coupon rate", "Zero until the bond matures"], 0, "When a bond trades at a discount, the investor earns the coupon plus the gain from price rising to face value at maturity. So YTM exceeds the coupon. A bond at a premium has YTM below its coupon."], ["Modes of Allotment other than Public Offers", "Under SEBI's share-based employee benefits rules, the minimum period between grant and vesting of employee stock options is:", ["Three months", "Six months", "Three years", "One year"], 3, "SEBI requires a minimum vesting period of one year from grant for options under a share-based employee benefit scheme. The company may set a longer vesting schedule."], ["Roles and Responsibilities in a Public Issue", "Monitoring the use of issue proceeds in a large IPO is done by a monitoring agency, which must be a:", ["Statutory auditor of the issuer company", "Credit rating agency registered with SEBI", "Registrar to the issue for that IPO", "Scheduled commercial bank acting as banker to the issue"], 1, "Since 2022, ICDR requires the monitoring agency for issues above the threshold to be a SEBI-registered credit rating agency. It reports on utilisation of proceeds against the stated objects."], ["Investor Interface with the R&T Agent", "A holder of physical shares has not registered bank account details with the RTA. Under current SEBI norms, how will his dividend be paid?", ["By a physical dividend warrant posted to his address", "In cash at the RTA's investor service centre", "By crediting it to his broker's client account", "Only electronically, after he furnishes the required KYC and bank details"], 3, "SEBI requires dividends and other payments to physical holders to be made only electronically once PAN, contact, bank details and specimen signature are registered. Payment is held until then."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "SEBI orders an inspection of an RTA's books and records. What is the RTA expected to do?", ["Produce the books, records and documents sought and cooperate with the inspecting officer", "Decline the inspection until a court order is produced", "Produce only those records that its issuer clients have approved in writing for inspection", "Provide only the audited annual accounts and nothing else"], 0, "SEBI's regulations oblige intermediaries to give inspecting officers access to books, records and documents and to cooperate fully. A court order or client approval is not required."], ["Introduction to Securities", "A listed company's continuing disclosure obligations, such as intimating record dates and filing shareholding patterns, mainly arise under:", ["SEBI (Issue of Capital and Disclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2018", "SEBI (Listing Obligations and Disclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2015", "SEBI (Substantial Acquisition of Shares and Takeovers) Regulations, 2011", "Foreign Exchange Management (Non-debt Instruments) Rules, 2019"], 1, "LODR Regulations set the ongoing obligations of listed entities after listing. ICDR governs the issue of capital, the Takeover Regulations deal with acquisitions, and the FEMA rules cover foreign investment."], ["Investor Interface with the R&T Agent", "An investor's signature on a request does not match the specimen in the RTA's records. The RTA should ask for:", ["A fresh demat account opening form", "Form ISR-2 with the signature attested by the investor's banker", "A copy of the company's annual report", "A transmission request supported by a death certificate"], 1, "SEBI norms allow the RTA to update a signature using Form ISR-2, in which the investor's banker attests the signature and confirms bank details. This prevents fraudulent requests."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "Investments in an Equity Linked Savings Scheme (ELSS) are subject to a lock-in period of:", ["One year", "Five years", "Seven years", "Three years"], 3, "ELSS units are locked in for three years from the date of allotment, the shortest lock-in among the traditional tax-saving options. Each SIP instalment has its own three-year lock-in."], ["Processes related to Depositories", "In a pledge of dematerialised shares, the pledge creation request is initiated by the:", ["Pledgee, without any involvement or consent of the pledgor", "RTA of the issuer company", "Stock exchange", "Pledgor, through its depository participant"], 3, "The pledgor (the borrower who owns the shares) initiates the pledge through its DP. The pledgee confirms the request through its own DP, after which the shares are marked as pledged."], ["Secondary Market Transactions", "In the settlement of exchange trades, 'pay-in' refers to:", ["The clearing corporation releasing funds and securities to members", "Investors paying dividends to the company", "The company paying listing fees to the exchange", "Clearing members delivering funds and securities to the clearing corporation"], 3, "Pay-in is when members deliver their obligations of funds and securities to the clearing corporation. Pay-out is the reverse, when the clearing corporation releases funds and securities to receiving members."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "An appeal against an order of the Securities Appellate Tribunal lies with:", ["The High Court of the state", "The SEBI board", "The Supreme Court of India", "The National Company Law Tribunal"], 2, "Under Section 15Z of the SEBI Act, a person aggrieved by a SAT decision may appeal to the Supreme Court on a question of law. SAT itself hears appeals against orders of SEBI."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "Sweat equity shares are issued by a company to:", ["Employees or directors for providing know-how, intellectual property or value additions", "Existing shareholders free of cost by capitalising the company's accumulated free reserves", "Qualified institutional buyers through a placement document", "Holders of convertible bonds on the date of conversion"], 0, "Sweat equity rewards employees or directors for know-how, IPR or value addition, at a discount or for non-cash consideration. Free shares to existing holders are bonus shares, and QIB placements are QIPs."], ["Introduction to Securities", "Government of India dated securities and treasury bills are issued, on behalf of the Government, by:", ["The Reserve Bank of India", "The Securities and Exchange Board of India", "The National Stock Exchange of India", "The Registrar of Companies"], 0, "The RBI acts as the debt manager of the Government and conducts auctions of G-secs and T-bills. SEBI regulates the securities market, while stock exchanges only provide trading platforms for these instruments."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "A company with shares of face value ₹2 declares a dividend of 150%. How much will a shareholder holding 600 shares receive (ignoring tax)?", ["₹180", "₹1,200", "₹18,000", "₹1,800"], 3, "Dividend percentages are applied to face value. 150% of ₹2 is ₹3 per share, and ₹3 × 600 shares = ₹1,800. Applying the rate to market price would be a common mistake."], ["Roles and Responsibilities in a Public Issue", "For IPOs on the SME platform, the lead manager must ensure compulsory market making for a minimum of:", ["Six months from listing", "One year from listing", "Ten years from listing or until migration to the main board", "Three years from listing"], 3, "ICDR requires market making for SME issues for at least three years from listing so that the shares have liquidity. The market maker gives two-way quotes for a minimum portion of the trading day."], ["Depository Services", "A Basic Services Demat Account (BSDA) is meant for:", ["Foreign portfolio investors who trade actively in equity derivatives", "Companies that hold treasury shares", "Brokers who hold clients' securities in a pool", "Individuals with a single demat account and small holdings, at reduced charges"], 3, "The BSDA is designed for small individual investors who have only one demat account and holdings below a value set by SEBI. Annual maintenance charges are nil or low, depending on holding value."], ["Public Offer of Securities", "In a book-built IPO, shares allocated to anchor investors come out of the:", ["QIB portion", "Retail individual investor portion", "Non-institutional investor portion", "Employee reservation portion"], 0, "Anchor investors are qualified institutional buyers, and up to 60% of the QIB portion may be allocated to them on a discretionary basis one working day before the issue opens."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "An Infrastructure Investment Trust (InvIT) typically holds:", ["Infrastructure assets such as toll roads and power transmission lines", "Listed equity shares of banks, NBFCs and insurance companies", "Gold and other precious metals", "Government treasury bills"], 0, "InvITs pool investor money to own revenue-generating infrastructure projects, such as roads, transmission lines and pipelines, and distribute cash flows to unit holders. They do not primarily hold shares, gold or T-bills."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "A credit rating assigned to a debenture issue primarily indicates:", ["The relative likelihood of timely payment of interest and principal", "The expected price at which the debenture will list on the exchange", "A guarantee by the rating agency that investors will be repaid", "The outlook for the issuer's equity share price over the next year"], 0, "A rating is an opinion on credit risk, that is, the chance of timely servicing of the debt. It is not a guarantee of repayment and says nothing about listing price or equity returns."], ["Investor Interface with the R&T Agent", "Shares are held jointly by Suresh and Kavita. Suresh dies, and Kavita asks the RTA to delete his name. This request is processed as:", ["Transmission in favour of the surviving holder", "Transposition of names", "Transfer by way of gift", "Rematerialisation of shares"], 0, "On the death of a joint holder, the deceased's name is deleted by transmission to the surviving holder, on submission of the death certificate. Transposition only changes the order of living holders."], ["Modes of Allotment other than Public Offers", "In a qualified institutions placement, allotment can NOT be made to:", ["Promoters or entities in the promoter group", "Mutual funds", "Foreign portfolio investors registered as QIBs", "Insurance companies"], 0, "ICDR prohibits allotment under a QIP, directly or indirectly, to promoters, the promoter group or related persons. Mutual funds, eligible FPIs and insurers are QIBs who may participate."], ["Processes related to Public Offering of Shares", "Which category of bidder is allowed to bid at the 'cut-off' price in a book-built IPO?", ["Qualified institutional buyers", "Retail individual investors", "Non-institutional investors bidding above ₹2 lakh", "Anchor investors"], 1, "Only retail individual investors (and eligible employees and shareholders in reserved portions within the limit) may bid at cut-off. QIBs and NIIs must bid at a specific price and cannot use the cut-off option."], ["Processes related to Public Offering of Shares", "An ASBA bidder in an IPO is not allotted any shares. What happens to the application money?", ["The blocked amount is unblocked in the bidder's bank account", "A refund cheque is posted to the bidder's address", "The amount is adjusted against the next IPO", "It is transferred to the issuer and refunded later by cheque"], 0, "Under ASBA, money never leaves the bidder's account until allotment. For unsuccessful bids, the bank simply releases the block on instruction, so no refund cheque or transfer is needed."], ["Processes related to Depositories", "Which of these is a common reason for an RTA to reject a demat request?", ["The investor's depository participant is a private sector bank", "The signature on the request does not match the specimen in the RTA's records", "The shares have a face value of ₹1 each", "The investor already holds other shares in demat"], 1, "RTAs verify certificates, names and signatures against their records. A signature mismatch, a name mismatch or certificates not matching the folio are typical reasons for rejection."], ["Modes of Allotment other than Public Offers", "A share trades at ₹100 before a 1:4 rights issue priced at ₹75. What is the theoretical ex-rights price?", ["₹87.50", "₹100", "₹95", "₹80"], 2, "Four old shares at ₹100 (₹400) plus one rights share at ₹75 give ₹475 for five shares. ₹475 ÷ 5 = ₹95, which is the theoretical ex-rights price."], ["Client Servicing", "A marketing firm asks an RTA for a list of shareholders' mobile numbers and email addresses for a promotion. The RTA should:", ["Share it for a fee, since the data belongs to the RTA", "Share it if the firm promises to use it carefully", "Share only email addresses, since they are not sensitive", "Decline, since investor data must be kept confidential and used only for authorised purposes"], 3, "RTAs hold investor data on behalf of issuers and must protect it. Sharing it with third parties for marketing would breach confidentiality obligations under SEBI norms and data protection law."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "A financial contract whose value depends on an underlying asset such as a share, index or commodity is a:", ["Debenture", "Derivative", "Depository receipt", "Treasury bill"], 1, "Futures and options are derivatives because their value is derived from an underlying. Debentures and T-bills are direct debt claims, and a depository receipt represents ownership of underlying shares."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "Which entity is responsible for the safekeeping of securities purchased by a mutual fund scheme?", ["The sponsor", "The registrar and transfer agent", "The custodian", "The distributor"], 2, "A SEBI-registered custodian holds the scheme's securities and handles their settlement and corporate actions. The RTA maintains unit holder records, the sponsor sets up the fund and distributors sell units."], ["Modes of Allotment other than Public Offers", "Under the Companies Act, 2013, a private placement offer can be made in a financial year to a maximum of how many persons, excluding QIBs and employees under ESOP?", ["50", "500", "200", "1,000"], 2, "Section 42 and the related rules limit a private placement offer to 200 persons in a financial year, excluding QIBs and employees offered shares under ESOP. Offers beyond this count are treated as public offers."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "Under the Companies Act, 2013, a company is NOT permitted to issue:", ["Cumulative preference shares", "Irredeemable preference shares", "Convertible preference shares", "Participating preference shares"], 1, "Section 55 prohibits issuing irredeemable preference shares; all preference shares must be redeemable within the prescribed period. Cumulative, convertible and participating features are permitted."], ["Investor Interface with the R&T Agent", "A holder of physical shares wishes to register a nominee for the first time. Which form is used?", ["Form SH-14", "Form SH-13", "Form ISR-3", "Form ISR-2"], 1, "SH-13 under the Companies Act rules is used to make a nomination. SH-14 is for cancelling or varying a nomination, ISR-3 for opting out of nomination and ISR-2 for banker's attestation of signature."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "Under the Companies Act, 2013, which of the following can NOT be used to issue fully paid bonus shares?", ["Free reserves", "Securities premium account", "Revaluation reserve", "Capital redemption reserve account"], 2, "Section 63 allows bonus shares out of free reserves, securities premium or capital redemption reserve, but specifically bars reserves created by revaluation of assets, since these are not realised gains."], ["Modes of Allotment other than Public Offers", "A shareholder neither applies for nor sells his rights entitlements before the rights issue closes. What happens to those entitlements?", ["They lapse and he gets no shares or money for them", "They are converted into bonus shares automatically", "The RTA applies on his behalf and debits his bank", "They are carried forward to the company's next rights issue"], 0, "Rights entitlements must be exercised or renounced within the issue period. Unused REs lapse and are extinguished, so a holder who wants value should sell them during the renunciation period."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "In a partly convertible debenture (PCD), what happens at the time of conversion?", ["The entire debenture converts into equity shares on the conversion date fixed at issue", "The holder receives only cash and no shares at all", "One part converts into equity shares and the remaining part is redeemed as debt", "The debenture is cancelled and replaced by preference shares"], 2, "A PCD has a convertible portion that becomes equity and a non-convertible portion that continues as debt and is redeemed in cash. A fully convertible debenture converts entirely into equity."], ["Roles and Responsibilities in a Public Issue", "In a public issue, the intermediary that agrees to subscribe to any shares left unsubscribed by the public is the:", ["Underwriter", "Registrar to the issue", "Sponsor bank", "Debenture trustee"], 0, "An underwriter commits to take up the shortfall in subscription, for a commission. The registrar processes applications, the sponsor bank handles UPI mandates and a debenture trustee serves debenture holders."], ["Public Offer of Securities", "In an IPO, the minimum promoters' contribution that must be locked in is generally:", ["5% of the pre-issue capital", "51% of the post-issue capital", "20% of the post-issue capital", "10% of the issue size"], 2, "ICDR requires promoters to contribute and lock in at least 20% of the post-issue capital, which gives promoters a continuing stake. Holdings beyond this minimum have a shorter lock-in."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "For a mutual fund, which activity is typically handled by its registrar and transfer agent?", ["Selecting securities for the scheme's portfolio and deciding when to sell them", "Approving the scheme's investment objective", "Processing purchases and redemptions and maintaining unit holder records", "Holding the scheme's securities in safe custody"], 2, "The MF RTA processes transactions, updates folios, issues account statements and handles investor service requests. Portfolio decisions are made by the AMC, and securities are held by the custodian."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "Ravi holds partly paid shares of face value ₹10 on which ₹4 has been paid up. If the company is wound up, Ravi:", ["Can be required to pay the unpaid ₹6 per share", "Has no further liability on these shares once wound up", "Must pay the full ₹10 per share again", "Becomes a creditor for ₹6 per share"], 0, "A shareholder's liability is limited to the unpaid amount on the shares held. Ravi has paid ₹4, so he can be called upon for the remaining ₹6 per share, but nothing beyond that."], ["Processes related to Public Offering of Shares", "For sending allotment communication to IPO applicants, the registrar takes their address and bank details from:", ["Details handwritten on the application form", "The issuer's register of members", "Depository records linked to the DP ID and client ID in the bid", "The lead manager's marketing database of prospective investors"], 2, "Demographic details for allotment and communication are fetched from the depositories using the DP ID, client ID and PAN given in the bid, which is why investors must keep demat records updated."], ["Introduction to Securities", "Which security combines features of both debt and equity?", ["A treasury bill", "A commercial paper", "A convertible debenture", "An ordinary equity share"], 2, "A convertible debenture pays interest like debt until it converts into equity shares, so it is a hybrid. Treasury bills and commercial paper are pure short-term debt, while equity shares carry only ownership features."], ["Processes related to Depositories", "The reconciliation of share capital audit, which ties total issued capital to holdings in NSDL, CDSL and physical form, is carried out:", ["Once in five years by the statutory auditor", "Every quarter by a practising chartered accountant or company secretary", "Only at the time of an IPO by the lead manager", "Every month by the stock exchange"], 1, "The Depositories and Participants Regulations require issuers to get this audit done every quarter by a qualified practising CA or CS and to submit the report to the stock exchanges."], ["Processes related to Public Offering of Shares", "A retail investor places an IPO bid using UPI but does not approve the mandate request in her UPI app before the cut-off. What is the result?", ["The broker pays the amount and recovers it after allotment", "The bid is considered and money is debited on allotment", "Funds are not blocked and the bid is not considered for allotment", "The registrar approves the mandate on her behalf after the issue closes"], 2, "Under the UPI mechanism, the investor must accept the mandate to block funds. Without approval, no funds are blocked and the bid is treated as invalid; nobody else can approve it for her."], ["Roles and Responsibilities in a Public Issue", "When an issue is to be listed on more than one exchange, the issuer chooses one of them, for allotment and related purposes, as the:", ["Principal clearing exchange", "Designated stock exchange", "Lead stock exchange", "Primary listing authority"], 1, "The issuer names a designated stock exchange in the offer document. The basis of allotment is finalised in consultation with it, and its representative is involved in allotment-related processes."], ["Client Servicing", "An investor remains dissatisfied after the RTA's response and the SCORES process. Which mechanism can he use next for conciliation or arbitration?", ["The online dispute resolution portal (SMART ODR)", "The Registrar of Companies", "The consumer helpline of the RBI", "The company's AGM"], 0, "SEBI's online dispute resolution framework lets investors start online conciliation and, if needed, arbitration against market participants, including RTAs, through the common ODR portal."], ["Depository Services", "Shares held in demat form do not carry distinctive numbers because they are:", ["Held only by the issuer company and not by individual investors", "Fungible, so any share of that ISIN is identical to any other", "Not entitled to corporate benefits", "Converted into debentures on credit to the account"], 1, "Demat securities of the same ISIN are fungible; ownership is recorded as a quantity rather than as specific numbered certificates. This makes transfer and settlement fast and avoids certificate-level tracking."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "An RTA employee learns that a client company's board will approve a large bonus issue tomorrow. Before announcement, the employee buys that company's shares. This is likely to violate:", ["SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996", "SEBI (Depositories and Participants) Regulations, 2018, as amended", "SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020", "SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015"], 3, "A bonus announcement is unpublished price sensitive information. Trading while in possession of UPSI breaches the PIT Regulations, which apply to connected persons such as RTA staff handling the company's work."], ["Depository Services", "A demat account holder going abroad for a long period wants to prevent any debit from her account. She can ask her DP to:", ["Close the account and reopen it later", "Rematerialise all her holdings", "Transfer holdings to her broker's pool account", "Freeze her account for debits"], 3, "Depositories let account holders freeze their accounts for debits, credits or both. Freezing debits protects holdings from unauthorised transfers while still allowing credits such as bonus shares."], ["Processes related to Depositories", "In rematerialisation of shares, the physical certificates are printed and dispatched to the investor by:", ["The depository participant", "The issuer's RTA", "The depository", "The clearing corporation"], 1, "The investor submits a remat request through the DP. The depository blocks the holding and informs the RTA, which prints and dispatches certificates and confirms, after which the demat balance is debited."], ["Investor Interface with the R&T Agent", "A shareholder holding only demat shares writes to the company's RTA to change her address. What should the RTA advise?", ["Submit Form ISR-1 to the RTA so that it can change the depository records", "Update the address through her DP, since the RTA uses depository records for demat holdings", "Convert the shares into physical form first", "Write to SEBI, which updates addresses for all investors"], 1, "For demat holdings, the RTA relies on beneficial owner details downloaded from the depositories. Address and bank changes must therefore be made through the DP, after which they flow to the company's records."], ["Roles and Responsibilities in a Public Issue", "A successful IPO applicant finds that allotted shares have not been credited to his demat account. Whom should he contact first?", ["The registrar to the issue", "The stock exchange's trading desk", "The Reserve Bank of India", "His mutual fund distributor"], 0, "The registrar handles post-issue matters such as allotment, demat credit and unblocking, and must redress such complaints. If not resolved, the investor can escalate through SEBI's grievance system."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "Which of the following is NOT an objective stated in the preamble to the SEBI Act, 1992?", ["Protecting the interests of investors in securities", "Promoting the development of the securities market", "Regulating the securities market", "Setting the policy repo rate"], 3, "The SEBI Act's preamble names investor protection, development and regulation of the securities market. The policy repo rate is set by the RBI's Monetary Policy Committee, not by SEBI."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "A company has 4.5 crore equity shares outstanding and its share price is ₹320. What is its market capitalisation?", ["₹1,440 crore", "₹144 crore", "₹14,400 crore", "₹1,280 crore"], 0, "Market capitalisation equals shares outstanding multiplied by market price: 4.5 crore × ₹320 = ₹1,440 crore. Face value is not used in this calculation."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "An entity that maintains the register of holders and processes transfers and transmissions for a company, but does not handle public issues, is acting as a:", ["Registrar to an issue", "Merchant banker", "Depository participant", "Share transfer agent"], 3, "A registrar to an issue handles application and allotment work for issues, while a share transfer agent maintains records and processes post-issue requests. An RTA may be registered to do one or both."], ["Secondary Market Transactions", "Under current SEBI norms, securities bought by a client in the cash segment are paid out:", ["First to the broker's pool account and later to the client", "To the RTA, which then credits the client", "To the depository's suspense account for seven days", "Directly to the client's demat account by the clearing corporation"], 3, "To protect client securities, SEBI mandated direct pay-out of securities by the clearing corporation to clients' demat accounts, instead of routing them through brokers' pool accounts."], ["Modes of Allotment other than Public Offers", "After a company issues bonus shares, which of the following remains unchanged?", ["Number of issued shares", "Paid-up share capital", "Total shareholders' funds (net worth)", "Balance in free reserves used for capitalisation"], 2, "A bonus issue moves an amount from reserves to share capital. Shares and paid-up capital rise and reserves fall by the same amount, so total net worth stays the same and no cash changes hands."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "Foreign Currency Convertible Bonds (FCCBs) issued by an Indian company are:", ["Rupee bonds issued in India that can be converted into shares of a foreign parent company", "Equity shares listed only on overseas exchanges", "Bonds denominated in a foreign currency that can be converted into the company's equity shares", "Short-term loans from foreign banks repayable in rupees"], 2, "FCCBs are raised overseas in foreign currency, pay interest like bonds and give holders the option to convert into equity of the Indian issuer at a set price. They are not equity shares or bank loans."], ["Client Servicing", "An RTA finds three deficiencies in a transmission request. What is the best practice?", ["Reject the request without giving any reasons", "Raise one deficiency at a time as each is fixed", "Inform the claimant of all the deficiencies together in a single communication", "Process it anyway and seek documents after transfer"], 2, "Listing all deficiencies at once avoids repeated rounds and delays. Processing without the required documents could cause wrongful transmission, and rejection without reasons is poor service."], ["Roles and Responsibilities in a Public Issue", "Under ICDR, the issuer must appoint as Registrar to the issue an entity that:", ["Is a subsidiary of the lead manager", "Holds at least 1% of the issuer's paid-up equity shares", "Is also the issuer's statutory auditor", "Has electronic connectivity with both depositories"], 3, "Since allotment is made only in demat form, ICDR requires the issuer to appoint a registrar with connectivity to both NSDL and CDSL. Shareholding in the issuer or links with the auditor are not requirements."], ["Introduction to Securities", "Capital market instruments are generally those whose original maturity is:", ["Up to 14 days only", "Exactly one year and not beyond", "More than one year, or with no fixed maturity", "Between 91 days and 364 days only, as fixed by the RBI"], 2, "The capital market deals in long-term funds: bonds and debentures with maturities above one year, and equity shares that have no maturity at all. Short-term paper of up to one year belongs to the money market."], ["Secondary Market Transactions", "Who can place orders directly in a stock exchange's trading system?", ["Any investor with a PAN", "The company's RTA", "Trading members registered with SEBI", "Depository participants that are banks"], 2, "Only trading members (stock brokers) registered with SEBI and admitted by the exchange can enter orders. Investors trade through these brokers, while RTAs and DPs have no trading access."], ["Depository Services", "For dematerialised shares, who is entitled to receive dividends and to vote at general meetings?", ["The depository, since it is the registered owner", "The beneficial owner as shown in the depository's records", "The depository participant that maintains the beneficial owner's account", "The RTA of the issuer company"], 1, "Although the depository is the registered owner, the Depositories Act gives all rights and benefits, including dividend and voting, to the beneficial owner. The depository has no voting rights on such shares."], ["Public Offer of Securities", "During the bid period, an issuer revises the price band. What must happen to the bid period?", ["It must close the same day and fresh bids must be invited", "It continues unchanged since only the price has moved", "It must be extended by at least three working days, subject to a total of ten", "It must be restarted from the first day, with a full fresh bid period for all categories"], 2, "ICDR requires the bid period to be extended by at least three additional working days after a price band revision, but the total bid period cannot exceed ten working days. The revision must also be publicised."], ["Depository Services", "To let a stock broker debit shares from a client's demat account for sales and pledges, SEBI now requires the client to give a:", ["Demat Debit and Pledge Instruction (DDPI)", "General power of attorney for all purposes", "Delivery instruction slip for every trade", "Letter of confirmation from the RTA"], 0, "The DDPI replaced the broad power of attorney and limits the broker's authority to specific purposes such as settlement of trades and margin pledges. It reduces the risk of misuse of client securities."], ["Public Offer of Securities", "In a book-built issue by a company eligible under Regulation 6(1) of ICDR, the portion for retail individual investors must be at least:", ["35% of the net offer", "10% of the net offer", "15% of the net offer", "50% of the net offer"], 0, "For companies meeting the profitability criteria, not less than 35% goes to retail individual investors, not less than 15% to non-institutional investors, and not more than 50% to QIBs."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "A bond of face value ₹1,000 with a 9% annual coupon is trading at ₹1,125. What is its current yield?", ["9.00%", "11.25%", "8.00%", "7.20%"], 2, "Current yield is annual coupon divided by market price. The coupon is ₹90 (9% of ₹1,000), so ₹90 ÷ ₹1,125 = 8.00%. The coupon rate of 9% is based on face value, not price."]]};
